<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-s09e01" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Episode 1: The xy-Plane</title>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 1 Learning Goals</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Interpret the xy-plane as two crossed number lines used to simultaneously represent the behavior of two different, but related, quantities 
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Plot ordered pairs on the xy-plane 
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Express points on the xy-plane as ordered pairs 
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 1 Key Terminology</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>xy</m>-plane
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Ordered pairs
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>x</m>-axis
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>y</m>-axis
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Coordinate
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <p>
    Suppose that you have two related quantities, one called <m>x</m> and one called <m>y</m>. For example, maybe you are buying apples, and each pound of apples costs $2. 
    If <m>x</m> represents the pounds of apples you buy, and <m>y</m> is the total amount you spend, then <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are related by the formula:
    <md>
      y = 2x
    </md>
    Each value of <m>x</m> is “paired” with an associated value of <m>y</m>. For example, when <m>x=1</m>, the formula forces 
    <md>
      y = 2(1) = 2
    </md>
    These pairings can be recorded in a table.
  </p>
  <tabular left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom="minor" halign="center">
    <row>
      <cell>Pounds of Apples (<m>x</m>)</cell>
      <cell>Total Cost (<m>y</m>)</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>0</cell>
      <cell>2(0)=0</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>1</cell>
      <cell>2(1)=2</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>2</cell>
      <cell>2(2)=4</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>3</cell>
      <cell>2(3)=6</cell>
    </row>
  </tabular>
  <p>
    We can also represent this relationship visually on what’s called the <term>xy-plane</term>. 
    The xy-plane consists of two crossed number lines, one horizontal and one vertical. 
  </p>
  <p>
    The horizontal line tracks the values of <m>x</m>, and it’s called the <term>x-axis</term>. 
  </p>
  <p>
    The vertical line tracks the values of <m>y</m>, and it’s called the <term>y-axis</term>. 
  </p>
  <figure>
        <caption>The xy-plane</caption>
        <image width="90%">
        <prefigure label="pf-xy-plane" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(500,500)" margins="5">
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      To reflect the fact that <m>x=1</m> is paired with <m>y=2</m>, we draw a dot on the xy-plane. 
      The value <m>x=1</m> tells us the dot’s horizontal position, and the value <m>y=2</m> tells us the dot’s vertical position. 
    </p>
    <p>
      The numbers <m>x=1</m> and <m>y=2</m> are called the dot’s <term>coordinates</term> because they tell you where the dot is located.
    </p>
    <figure>
        <caption>The pairing of x = 1 and y = 2 represented on the plane by a dot.</caption>
        <image width="90%">
        <prefigure label="pf-1-2-example" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(500,500)" margins="5">
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        <point p="(1,2)" stroke="blue" fill="blue" size="6"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      If we drew a dot for every pairing in the table, it would look like this:
    </p>
    <figure>
        <caption>The four pairings in the table, represented on the plane by dots</caption>
        <image width="90%">
        <prefigure label="pf-2x-example" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(500,500)" margins="5">
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        <point p="(0,0)" stroke="blue" fill="blue" size="6"/>
          <point p="(1,2)" stroke="blue" fill="blue" size="6"/>
            <point p="(2,4)" stroke="blue" fill="blue" size="6"/>
              <point p="(3,6)" stroke="blue" fill="blue" size="6"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      When working with related quantities, we write the pairings using this <term>ordered pair</term> notation:
      <md>
        (x,y)
      </md>
      For example, the pairings in the table would be written
      <md>
        (0,0)
      </md>
      <md>
        (1,2)
      </md>
      <md>
        (2,4)
      </md>
      <md>
        (3,6)
      </md>
    </p>
    <assemblage>
      <title>Be Careful!</title>
      <p>
        The notation used for ordered pairs is identical to the notation used for intervals in Season 8. 
        You need to use context to tell the difference between an ordered pair and an interval.
      </p>
      <p>
        Ordered pairs show up when you are dealing with equations like <m>y=2x</m> that have two related variables.
      </p>
      <p>
        Intervals show up when you are dealing with inequalities like <m>1 \lt x \lt 2</m> that have only one variable.
      </p>
    </assemblage>
    <example>
      <p>
        Draw the point (-1,4) on the xy-plane
      </p>
      <p>
        Solution: The horizontal position of the point is given by the first number, <m>x=-1</m>. 
        The vertical position of the point is given by the second number, <m>y=4</m>.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <caption>A plot of the point (-1,4) on the xy-plane</caption>
        <image width="90%">
        <prefigure label="pf-point-example1" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(500,500)" margins="5">
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        <point p="(-1,4)" stroke="blue" fill="blue" size="6"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
    </figure>
    </example>
     <example>
      <p>
        Draw the point (-3,0) on the xy-plane
      </p>
      <p>
        Solution: The horizontal position of the point is given by the first number, <m>x=-3</m>. 
        The vertical position of the point is given by the second number, <m>y=0</m>.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <caption>A plot of the point (-3,0) on the xy-plane</caption>
        <image width="90%">
        <prefigure label="pf-point-example2" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(500,500)" margins="5">
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        <point p="(-3,0)" stroke="blue" fill="blue" size="6"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
    </figure>
    </example>
     <example>
      <p>
        Draw the point (0,2) on the xy-plane
      </p>
      <p>
        Solution: The horizontal position of the point is given by the first number, <m>x=0</m>. 
        The vertical position of the point is given by the second number, <m>y=2</m>.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <caption>A plot of the point (0,2) on the xy-plane</caption>
        <image width="90%">
        <prefigure label="pf-point-example3" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(500,500)" margins="5">
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        <point p="(0,2)" stroke="blue" fill="blue" size="6"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
    </figure>
    </example>
    <worksheet xml:id="ws-s09e01">
      <title>Season 9, Episode 1 Practice Problems</title>
      <exercise>
       <introduction>
        <p>
          For the relationship <m>y = x^2</m>
        </p>
       </introduction>
       <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Fill out the following table
          </p>
          <tabular left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom = "minor" halign="center">
            <row>
              <cell><m>x</m></cell>
              <cell><m>y</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>-2</cell>
              <cell><m>\hspace{0.5in}</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>-1</cell>
              <cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>0</cell>
              <cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>1</cell>
              <cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>2</cell>
              <cell></cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </statement>
       </task>
       <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Draw all the pairs in the table together on a single xy-plane.
          </p>
          <image width="70%">
            <shortdescription>A blank xy-plane</shortdescription>
            <prefigure label="pf-xy-plane-ex2" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(500,500)" margins="5">
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
          </image>
        </statement>
       </task>
      </exercise>
      <p>
        Write the points in each of the following drawings as an ordered pair <m>(x,y)</m>.
      </p>

      <sidebyside widths="35% 35%">
          <exercise>
          <image>
        <prefigure label="pf-913a" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(500,500)" margins="5">
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        <point p="(4,1)" stroke="blue" fill="blue" size="6"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
            
          </exercise>
          <exercise>
           
              <image>
        <prefigure label="pf-913b" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(500,500)" margins="5">
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        <point p="(-1,-2)" stroke="blue" fill="blue" size="6"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
            
          </exercise>
        </sidebyside>
         <sidebyside widths="35% 35%">
          <exercise>
            
              <image>
        <prefigure label="pf-913c" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(500,500)" margins="5">
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        <point p="(0,3)" stroke="blue" fill="blue" size="6"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
            
          </exercise>
          <exercise>
           
              <image>
        <prefigure label="pf-913d" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(500,500)" margins="5">
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        <point p="(3,-4)" stroke="blue" fill="blue" size="6"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
            
          </exercise>
        </sidebyside>
      
          <p>
            Plot each of the following points on a different copy of the xy-plane.
          </p>

        <sidebyside>
          <exercise workspace="1.75in">
               <p>
                (-4,4)
              </p>
            </exercise>
         <exercise workspace="1.75in">
               <p>
                (3,-3)
              </p>
            </exercise>
            <exercise workspace="1.75in">
               <p>
                (-2,-4)
              </p>
            </exercise>
        </sidebyside>
    <sidebyside>
          <exercise workspace="1.75in">
               <p>
                (2,-1)
              </p>
            </exercise>
